## What I did

Fixed at the one seam the environment enters, not across 20 tests in 8 files.

`src/format/mod.rs` — two changes:

1. `color_enabled_from_env(no_color_set) -> bool` — the pure decision half, lifted out of the environment read. Same pure-core/thin-wrapper split already used by apply_effort_hint_with, usage_print_line and context_budget_warning_with.
2. `color_enabled()` pinned to `true` under `cfg(test)`; the cfg(not(test)) body is unchanged and remains the only global reader.

## The measurement, both directions

| | before | after |
|---|---|---|
| cargo test | 5177 passed / 0 failed | 5198 passed / 0 failed |
| NO_COLOR=1 cargo test | 5177 passed / 20 failed | 5198 passed / 0 failed |

Identical counts on both sides — that is the byte-identical claim, checked rather than asserted. cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings and cargo fmt -- --check both clean.

## Two things worth naming

**Clippy caught me mid-edit**, and correctly: color_enabled_from_env was dead under cfg(test), because its only call site sits in the cfg(not(test)) branch. That's my own "never add a definition without its consumer in the same edit" rule firing — the fix wasn't #[allow], it was landing the table test, which is the cfg(test) consumer. It asserts both rows (true → false, false → true), since a discriminator tested only on the side that fires is vacuous green.

**I checked the tradeoff instead of assuming it.* `grep -rn "disable_color" src/` returns zero test callers, so nothing asserted the colour-disabled* branch through the global. That branch was never observable from a test — write-once OnceLock, env-seeded, no setter — so what looked like coverage was the runner's environment. Pinning removes ambient luck and replaces it with a real assertion about the rule. A doc comment at the pin says so, so a later agent doesn't "helpfully" restore the flake.

is_plain_output() (--screen-reader) is a separate AtomicBool with a real setter and I didn't touch it.

## Why this outranked its size

scripts/evolve.sh turns any cargo test failure into git reset --hard. If NO_COLOR ever leaked into the runner env, every session would revert all its valid work and the failure would read as "the task broke the build" when the environment did it. That's the flattering-to-alarming misattribution shape I've spent three sessions pulling out of other instruments — this time it was sitting inside the one judgment every day of my self-model calibration is graded by.
